\title{The Corrected Product:\\ Normalized $q$-Series Form a Torsion-Free Divisible Group}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 24, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A formal Laurent series $f \in \CC((q))$ is called \emph{normalized} if it has the shape
$f(q) = q^{-1} + a_0 + a_1 q + a_2 q^2 + \cdots$, that is, a simple pole at $q = 0$ with
residue $1$ and no lower-order terms. This is the normalization carried by the modular
invariant $J = j - 744$ and by all $194$ McKay--Thompson series of Monstrous Moonshine.
Normalized series are not closed under multiplication: a product of $m$ of them has a pole
of order exactly $m$. We make this obstruction precise and then dissolve it. We prove that
$q^{k} f_1 \cdots f_m$ is normalized if and only if $k = m-1$, so the correction is unique;
we introduce the \emph{corrected product} $f \star g := q f g$ and show that the normalized
series form a commutative group under $\star$ with identity $q^{-1}$, canonically isomorphic
to the group of $1$-units of $\CC[[q]]$; we prove the structure theorem
$\CC((q))^{\times} \cong \ZZ \times \CC[[q]]^{\times}$ exhibiting the pole order as a direct
summand; and we prove that the corrected group is \emph{torsion free} (a geometric-sum
argument) and \emph{divisible} (via substitution into the binomial series $(1+X)^{1/n}$),
hence uniquely divisible, hence a $\QQ$-vector space in multiplicative notation.
Consequences include closed-form Newton identities for the coefficients of corrected
inverses and roots; existence and uniqueness of rational corrected powers $f^{\star r}$;
the fact that each power map is a group automorphism; and the existence of a unique
normalized $G$ with $q^{193} G^{194} = q^{193} \prod_g T_g$, the canonical \emph{geometric
mean} of the Monster's trace functions. We also record a finite-determinacy theorem for
coefficients of products, and exact numerical evidence that the corrected square root of
$J$ has integer coefficients through $q^{10}$ while its cube and fifth roots have
denominators that are unbounded powers of $3$ and $5$ respectively.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} normalized $q$-series, Laurent series, pole order, $1$-units,
divisible abelian groups, binomial series, McKay--Thompson series, Monstrous Moonshine.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Normalization as a convention with consequences}

The theory of modular functions is full of normalizations, and Monstrous Moonshine is built
on one of them. Every McKay--Thompson series $T_g$ attached to a conjugacy class $g$ of the
Monster group $\mathbb{M}$ is expanded as a $q$-series of the specific shape
\[
  T_g(q) = q^{-1} + a_0(g) + a_1(g)\, q + a_2(g)\, q^2 + \cdots,
\]
and the principal one, $T_1 = J = j - 744$, has
\begin{equation}\label{eq:J}
  J(q) = q^{-1} + 196884\,q + 21493760\,q^2 + 864299970\,q^3 + \cdots.
\end{equation}
The normalization does two things at once: it fixes the additive ambiguity (by prescribing
the constant term, in the moonshine convention $a_0 = 0$) and it fixes the multiplicative
ambiguity (by prescribing that the polar part is exactly $q^{-1}$). Without the latter, the
statement ``$196884 = 196883 + 1$'' would be meaningless, because the coefficients would
only be defined up to a common scalar.

Because the normalization is a multiplicative condition, it is natural to ask whether the
normalized series form an algebraic structure under multiplication. They do not, and the
failure is exact rather than approximate. This paper isolates the failure, shows that it is
a single integer parameter, corrects it canonically, and then determines the resulting
group completely: it is torsion free and divisible, hence uniquely divisible, hence --- as
an abstract group --- a $\QQ$-vector space.

\subsection{Summary of results}

Throughout, $\CC[[q]]$ denotes formal power series, $\CC((q))$ formal Laurent series
(finitely many negative-exponent terms), and $\ord(f) \in \ZZ \cup \{\infty\}$ the order of
$f$, the least exponent with nonzero coefficient.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Normalization characterization} (Theorem~\ref{thm:charac}): $f$ is
  normalized iff $f = q^{-1}u$ for a unique $1$-unit $u$.
  \item \textbf{Pole order and unique correction} (Theorems~\ref{thm:poleorder} and
  \ref{thm:unique-exponent}): a product of $m$ normalized series has order $-m$ with leading
  coefficient $1$, and $q^{k} f_1 \cdots f_m$ is normalized iff $k = m-1$.
  \item \textbf{Corrected product and group law} (Theorems~\ref{thm:star} and
  \ref{thm:group}).
  \item \textbf{Structure of the unit group} (Theorem~\ref{thm:structure}).
  \item \textbf{Torsion freeness} (Theorem~\ref{thm:torsionfree}).
  \item \textbf{Divisibility} (Theorem~\ref{thm:divisible}).
  \item \textbf{Unique roots and power automorphisms} (Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqueroot},
  Corollary~\ref{cor:auto}).
  \item \textbf{$\QQ$-vector space structure and rational powers}
  (Theorems~\ref{thm:qvs} and \ref{thm:ratpow}).
  \item \textbf{Newton identities} (Theorems~\ref{thm:inv} and \ref{thm:sqrt}).
  \item \textbf{Finite determinacy} (Theorem~\ref{thm:determinacy}).
  \item \textbf{The moonshine mean} (Theorem~\ref{thm:mean}).
\end{enumerate}

Section~\ref{sec:computational} reports exact rational computations: the corrected square
root of $J$ has integer Laurent coefficients through $q^{10}$; the corrected cube root has
denominators $3, 9, 81$; the fifth root has denominators up to $5^{6}$ in the same range.

\section{Setting and the normalization characterization}

\subsection{Laurent and power series}

Let $\CC[[q]]$ be the ring of formal power series and $\CC((q))$ its field of fractions, the
field of formal Laurent series: elements are functions $\ZZ \to \CC$, $k \mapsto f_k$, whose
support is bounded below. Write $f = \sum_k f_k q^k$ and $[q^k]f := f_k$. Multiplication is
the Cauchy product, well defined because supports are bounded below.

The \emph{order} $\ord(f)$ of a nonzero $f$ is $\min\{k : f_k \ne 0\}$, with
$\ord(0) = \infty$. It is a discrete valuation:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:val}
  \ord(fg) = \ord(f) + \ord(g), \qquad \ord(f+g) \ge \min(\ord f, \ord g),
\end{equation}
and $\CC((q))$ is a field. The \emph{pole order} of $f$ is $-\ord(f)$ when positive.

A power series $u$ is a \emph{$1$-unit} if $[q^0]u = 1$. Write
$\Uone := 1 + q\,\CC[[q]]$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:oneunit}
$\Uone$ is a subgroup of $\CC[[q]]^{\times}$: it is closed under multiplication since
$[q^0](uv) = [q^0]u\cdot[q^0]v$, and if $u \in \Uone$ the recursion $v_0 = 1$,
$v_k = -\sum_{j=1}^{k} u_j v_{k-j}$ defines $v \in \Uone$ with $uv = 1$. Moreover
$\CC[[q]]^{\times} = \{u : [q^0]u \ne 0\} = \CC^{\times}\cdot \Uone$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{Normalized series}

\begin{definition}[Normalized $q$-series]\label{def:norm}
$f \in \CC((q))$ is \emph{normalized} if $[q^{-1}]f = 1$ and $[q^{k}]f = 0$ for all
$k < -1$; equivalently $f = q^{-1} + a_0 + a_1 q + \cdots$. Write $\Norm$ for the set of
normalized series. Every normalized $f$ has $\ord(f) = -1$ with leading coefficient $1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization characterization]\label{thm:charac}
$f \in \CC((q))$ is normalized if and only if $f = q^{-1}u$ for some $u \in \Uone$; the
series $u = qf$ is then unique.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplication by $q^{-1}$ shifts coefficients: $[q^{k}](q^{-1}u) = [q^{k+1}]u$, which
vanishes for $k < -1$ and equals $1$ at $k = -1$. Conversely, if $f$ is normalized then
$u := qf$ satisfies $[q^{k}]u = [q^{k-1}]f = 0$ for $k < 0$, so $u \in \CC[[q]]$, and
$[q^0]u = [q^{-1}]f = 1$. Uniqueness holds because $q$ is invertible in $\CC((q))$.
\end{proof}

We call $u = qf$ the \emph{normalized part} of $f$, so that $a_j = [q^{j+1}]u$ for $j \ge 0$.

\begin{definition}[Moonshine-shaped series]\label{def:trace}
For $c : \NN \to \CC$ with $c(0) = 0$, the trace series
$T_c(q) := q^{-1} + \sum_{n\ge 1} c(n) q^{n}$ is normalized. The modular invariant $J$ of
\eqref{eq:J} is the case $c(n) = \dim V^{\natural}_n$. The Monster has $194$ conjugacy
classes, hence a family $(T_g)_g$ of $194$ normalized series.
\end{definition}

\section{The pole-order obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Pole order of a finite product]\label{thm:poleorder}
Let $f_1,\dots,f_m$ be normalized, $m \ge 1$. Then
$\ord\big(\prod_{i} f_i\big) = -m$ and $[q^{-m}]\prod_i f_i = 1$. In particular the product
is normalized if and only if $m = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Orders add by \eqref{eq:val}, so the order is $-m$; the leading coefficient of a product is
the product of leading coefficients, all $1$. Normalization requires order $-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Shifting]\label{lem:shift}
For $k \in \ZZ$ and $f \ne 0$, $\ord(q^{k}f) = k + \ord(f)$, with the leading coefficient
unchanged.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the correcting exponent]\label{thm:unique-exponent}
Let $f_1,\dots,f_m$ be normalized and $k \in \ZZ$. Then $q^{k}f_1\cdots f_m$ is normalized
if and only if $k = m-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:poleorder} and Lemma~\ref{lem:shift}, $q^{k}\prod_i f_i$ has order
$k - m$ and leading coefficient $1$. Normalization is equivalent to order $-1$ together with
leading coefficient $1$; the latter is automatic, so the condition is $k - m = -1$.
\end{proof}

The $194$-fold moonshine instance reads: $q^{193}\prod_g T_g$ is normalized, and $193$ is
the only exponent with that property.

\section{The corrected product and the group of normalized series}

\begin{definition}[Corrected product]\label{def:star}
For $f,g \in \CC((q))$ set $f \star g := q\,f\,g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:star}
The operation $\star$ is commutative and associative on $\CC((q))$; the element $q^{-1}$
satisfies $q^{-1}\star f = f$; and if $f,g$ are normalized then so is $f\star g$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(f\star g)\star h = q(qfg)h = q^{2}fgh = f\star(g\star h)$, and $q^{-1}\star f = f$ is
immediate. Closure is Theorem~\ref{thm:unique-exponent} with $m = 2$, $k = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Group identification]\label{thm:group}
The map $\Phi : \Norm \to \Uone$, $\Phi(f) = qf$, is a bijection with
$\Phi(f\star g) = \Phi(f)\Phi(g)$ and $\Phi(q^{-1}) = 1$. Hence $(\Norm, \star, q^{-1})$ is
a commutative group, isomorphic to the group of $1$-units of $\CC[[q]]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Bijectivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:charac}; multiplicativity is $q(qfg) = (qf)(qg)$. Transport
of structure along a bijection turns the group $\Uone$ of Lemma~\ref{lem:oneunit} into a
group law on $\Norm$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The correct statement of the ``normalization obstruction'' is not that $\Norm$ fails to be a
monoid, but that $\Norm$ is a \emph{torsor}: it is the coset $q^{-1}\Uone$ of the subgroup
$\Uone \le \CC((q))^{\times}$, and $\star$ is the group law transported to that coset. The
factor $q^{-1}$ is a change of base point.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Powers and finite products]\label{prop:powers}
For $f_1,\dots,f_m \in \Norm$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:powers}
  f_1\star\cdots\star f_m = q^{\,m-1}f_1\cdots f_m, \qquad\text{in particular}\qquad
  f^{\star n} = q^{\,n-1}f^{n}.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $m$: $q^{k-1}f_1\cdots f_k \star f_{k+1} = q^{k}f_1\cdots f_{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Structure of the unit group of $\CC((q))$}

\begin{theorem}[Structure theorem]\label{thm:structure}
The map $\Psi : \ZZ \times \CC[[q]]^{\times} \to \CC((q))^{\times}$,
$\Psi(k,v) = q^{k}v$ (with $\ZZ$ written multiplicatively), is a group isomorphism, and
$\ord(\Psi(k,v)) = k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Psi$ is a homomorphism since $q^{k}v\cdot q^{l}w = q^{k+l}vw$. Injectivity: if
$q^{k}v = 1$, taking orders gives $k + \ord(v) = 0$; but $\ord(v) = 0$ for a unit of
$\CC[[q]]$, so $k = 0$ and $v = 1$. Surjectivity: for $f \ne 0$ set $k := \ord(f)$; then
$q^{-k}f$ is a power series with nonzero constant term, hence a unit, and
$f = \Psi(k, q^{-k}f)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:split}
$\ord : \CC((q))^{\times}\to\ZZ$ is a split surjective homomorphism with kernel
$\CC[[q]]^{\times}$, and $\CC[[q]]^{\times}\cong \CC^{\times}\times \Uone$ via
$v \mapsto ([q^0]v,\ v/[q^0]v)$. Hence
$\CC((q))^{\times}\cong \ZZ\times\CC^{\times}\times \Uone$.
\end{corollary}

Normalization pins the first two coordinates to $(-1, 1)$; the corrected product is exactly
the operation respecting that pinning.

\section{Torsion freeness, divisibility, unique roots}

\begin{theorem}[Torsion freeness of $\Uone$]\label{thm:torsionfree}
If $u \in \Uone$ and $u^{n} = 1$ for some $n \ge 1$, then $u = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In $\CC[[q]]$ we have $\big(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}u^{i}\big)(u-1) = u^{n}-1 = 0$. The ring
$\CC[[q]]$ is an integral domain, so one factor vanishes. The constant term of
$\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}u^{i}$ is $n \ne 0$, so that factor is nonzero; hence $u = 1$.
\end{proof}

Both hypotheses used --- integral domain, and $n \ne 0$ in the coefficient field --- hold in
characteristic $0$ and fail in characteristic $p$, where $(1+q)^{p} = 1 + q^{p}$ produces
torsion after a suitable substitution.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:normtorsion}
$\Norm$ is torsion free: $f^{\star n} = q^{-1}$ with $n \ge 1$, i.e.\ $q^{n-1}f^{n} = q^{-1}$,
forces $f = q^{-1}$.
\end{corollary}

For $r \in \QQ$ let
$B_r(X) := (1+X)^{r} = \sum_{k \ge 0}\binom{r}{k}X^{k} \in \QQ[[X]]$, where
$\binom{r}{k} = r(r-1)\cdots(r-k+1)/k!$.

\begin{lemma}[Exponent law]\label{lem:expl}
$B_rB_s = B_{r+s}$ for all $r,s \in \QQ$; in particular $B_r^{\,m} = B_{mr}$ for
$m \in \NN$, and $B_1 = 1 + X$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Vandermonde--Chu convolution $\sum_{i+j=k}\binom{r}{i}\binom{s}{j} = \binom{r+s}{k}$ is a
polynomial identity in $r,s$ valid for nonnegative integers, hence for all rationals.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Substitution]\label{lem:subst}
If $h \in \CC[[q]]$ has $[q^0]h = 0$, then $F \mapsto F(h) := \sum_k F_k h^{k}$ is a
well-defined ring homomorphism $\CC[[X]]\to\CC[[q]]$ (each coefficient is a finite sum since
$\ord(h^{k}) \ge k$), and $[q^0]F(h) = F_0$; in particular substitution maps $1$-units to
$1$-units.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Divisibility of $\Uone$]\label{thm:divisible}
For every $u \in \Uone$ and $n \ge 1$ there is $v \in \Uone$ with $v^{n} = u$, namely
\[
  v = B_{1/n}(u-1) = \sum_{k\ge 0}\binom{1/n}{k}(u-1)^{k}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $h := u - 1$, which has zero constant term, so substitution at $h$ is a ring
homomorphism by Lemma~\ref{lem:subst}, and $v := B_{1/n}(h) \in \Uone$. Applying that
homomorphism to $B_{1/n}^{\,n} = B_{1} = 1 + X$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:expl}) gives
$v^{n} = 1 + h = u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique divisibility of $\Uone$]\label{thm:uniquediv}
For every $u \in \Uone$ and $n \ge 1$ there is a unique $v \in \Uone$ with $v^{n} = u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence is Theorem~\ref{thm:divisible}. If $v_1^{n} = v_2^{n} = u$ then
$t := v_1v_2^{-1} \in \Uone$ satisfies $t^{n} = 1$, so $t = 1$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:torsionfree}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique corrected roots]\label{thm:uniqueroot}
Let $f$ be normalized and $n \ge 1$. There is exactly one normalized $g$ with
$g^{\star n} = f$, equivalently exactly one normalized $g$ with
\[
  q^{\,n-1}\,g(q)^{n} = f(q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Transport Theorem~\ref{thm:uniquediv} along $\Phi$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:group}) and rewrite
$g^{\star n}$ using \eqref{eq:powers}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Power automorphisms]\label{cor:auto}
For $n \ge 1$ the map $\pi_n(g) := g^{\star n}$ is an automorphism of $\Norm$: a
homomorphism because $\Norm$ is abelian, injective by Corollary~\ref{cor:normtorsion}, and
surjective by Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqueroot}. In particular $\Norm$ is a divisible abelian
group.
\end{corollary}

\section{The $\QQ$-vector space structure}

\begin{definition}
An abelian group $(M,+)$ is \emph{uniquely divisible} if $x \mapsto nx$ is a bijection
$M \to M$ for every $n \ge 1$; write $x/n$ for the unique preimage.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniquely divisible implies $\QQ$-vector space]\label{thm:qvs}
Let $M$ be uniquely divisible. Then $r\cdot x := p\,(x/d)$ for $r = p/d$ with $d \ge 1$ is
well defined and makes $M$ a $\QQ$-vector space; this is the unique $\QQ$-module structure
compatible with the given addition.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Well-definedness: if $p/d = p'/d'$ then $dd'\big(p(x/d)\big) = pd'x = p'dx =
dd'\big(p'(x/d')\big)$, and multiplication by $dd'$ is injective. Additivity in $x$ holds
because $x \mapsto x/d$ is the inverse of a homomorphism. Additivity in $r$ and
associativity $(rs)\cdot x = r\cdot(s\cdot x)$ follow by clearing denominators and using
injectivity. Uniqueness: any $\QQ$-structure satisfies $d(r\cdot x) = (dr)\cdot x$, which
determines $r \cdot x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rational corrected powers]\label{thm:ratpow}
For $f \in \Norm$ and $r \in \QQ$ define $f^{\star r}$ by the $\QQ$-action of
Theorem~\ref{thm:qvs} on the additive avatar of $\Norm$. Then
\[
  f^{\star(r+s)} = f^{\star r}\star f^{\star s}, \qquad
  f^{\star(rs)} = \big(f^{\star s}\big)^{\star r}, \qquad
  f^{\star 1} = f, \qquad f^{\star 0} = q^{-1},
\]
$f^{\star n}$ agrees with the $n$-fold corrected power for $n \in \NN$, and for
$r = p/d$ in lowest terms with $d \ge 1$ the element $f^{\star r}$ is the unique normalized
$g$ with $g^{\star d} = f^{\star p}$. Concretely
$f^{\star r} = q^{-1}B_r(qf - 1)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Newton identities for inverses and roots}

Let $f = q^{-1} + a_0 + a_1q + a_2q^{2}+\cdots$ be normalized, with normalized part
$u = qf = 1 + a_0q + a_1q^{2} + a_2q^{3} + \cdots$.

\begin{theorem}[Corrected inverse]\label{thm:inv}
If $g = q^{-1} + b_0 + b_1q + \cdots$ is normalized with $g \star f = q^{-1}$, then
\[
  b_0 = -a_0, \qquad b_1 = a_0^{2} - a_1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$g \star f = q^{-1}$ says $(qg)(qf) = 1$. The coefficients of $q^{1}$ and $q^{2}$ in the
Cauchy product give $b_0 + a_0 = 0$ and $b_1 + b_0a_0 + a_1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Corrected square root]\label{thm:sqrt}
If $g = q^{-1} + b_0 + b_1q + b_2q^{2}+\cdots$ is normalized with $g \star g = f$, i.e.\
$qg^{2} = f$, then
\[
  b_0 = \frac{a_0}{2}, \qquad
  b_1 = \frac{a_1}{2} - \frac{a_0^{2}}{8}, \qquad
  b_2 = \frac{a_2}{2} - \frac{a_0a_1}{4} + \frac{a_0^{3}}{16}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(qg)^{2} = qf$; comparing coefficients of $q^{1},q^{2},q^{3}$ gives $2b_0 = a_0$,
$2b_1 + b_0^{2} = a_1$, $2b_2 + 2b_0b_1 = a_2$, and back-substitution finishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $a_0 = 0$, the convention for all McKay--Thompson series, then $b_0 = 0$,
$b_1 = a_1/2$, and $b_2 = a_2/2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
For $J$ of \eqref{eq:J} the corrected square root begins
$q^{-1} + 98442\,q + 10746880\,q^{2}+\cdots$ and the corrected inverse begins
$q^{-1} - 196884\,q + \cdots$.
\end{example}

\section{Finite determinacy}

\begin{theorem}[Finite determinacy for products]\label{thm:determinacy}
Let $f_1,\dots,f_m$ and $g_1,\dots,g_m$ be normalized, $N \in \NN$, and suppose
$[q^{k}]f_i = [q^{k}]g_i$ for all $i$ and all $-1 \le k \le N$. Then
$[q^{k}]\prod_i f_i = [q^{k}]\prod_i g_i$ for all $k \le N - m + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pass to normalized parts $u_i = qf_i$, $v_i = qg_i$, which agree through degree $N+1$. For
power series, the coefficient of $q^{d}$ in a product depends only on the factors'
coefficients in degrees $\le d$; induction over the $m$ factors gives agreement of
$\prod u_i$ and $\prod v_i$ through degree $N+1$, and $\prod f_i = q^{-m}\prod u_i$ shifts
degrees down by $m$.
\end{proof}

Thus to know a $194$-fold moonshine product through $q^{N}$ one needs each factor through
$q^{N+193}$.

\section{Monstrous Moonshine applications}

Let $T_1,\dots,T_{194}$ be normalized series of moonshine shape, one per conjugacy class of
the Monster.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:moonprod}
The series $q^{193}\prod_{i=1}^{194}T_i$ is normalized, and $q^{k}\prod_iT_i$ is normalized
if and only if $k = 193$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
There is exactly one normalized $g$ with $q\,g(q)^{2} = J(q)$, and its expansion begins
$q^{-1} + 98442\,q + 10746880\,q^{2} + \cdots$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric mean of the Monster's trace functions]\label{thm:mean}
There is exactly one normalized $G$ with
\[
  q^{193}\,G(q)^{194} \;=\; q^{193}\prod_{i=1}^{194}T_i(q),
\]
namely $G = \big(\stbig_i T_i\big)^{\star(1/194)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The right-hand side is normalized by Theorem~\ref{thm:moonprod}; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqueroot} with $n = 194$.
\end{proof}

In the uncorrected setting the $194$-th root of a product of moonshine series is ill-posed
twice over: the product is not normalized, and roots would be ambiguous up to $194$-th roots
of unity. Correcting removes the first problem; torsion freeness removes the second.

\begin{remark}
More generally, for rational weights $r_i \in \QQ$ the weighted mean
$\stbig_i T_i^{\star r_i}$ exists and is unique (Theorem~\ref{thm:ratpow}).
\end{remark}

\section{Computational evidence: integrality of corrected roots of $J$}
\label{sec:computational}

All computations are exact over $\QQ$, using $c(1),\dots,c(10)$ of $J$:
$196884$, $21493760$, $864299970$, $20245856256$, $333202640600$, $4252023300096$,
$44656994071935$, $401490886656000$, $3176440229784420$, $22567393309593600$.

\paragraph{Corrected square root.} The unique normalized $g$ with $qg^{2} = J$ has
\[
\begin{aligned}
&[q^{0}] = 0, \quad [q^{1}] = 98442, \quad [q^{2}] = 10746880, \quad [q^{3}] = -4413263697,\\
&[q^{4}] = -1047821432832, \quad [q^{5}] = 376869391313174, \\
&[q^{6}] = 150580578862513152, \quad [q^{7}] = -35577391320709928685,\\
&[q^{8}] = -23497935558209789278208, \quad
[q^{9}] = 2998297272257446799809386,
\end{aligned}
\]
and $[q^{10}] = 3754973355232751413790773248$. All are integers, through the limit of the
input data. This is unexpected: $[q^{k}]$ of a square root is a polynomial in
$c(1),\dots,c(k+1)$ with denominators that are powers of $2$ growing like $2^{2k}$
(cf.\ the $1/8$ and $1/16$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:sqrt}).

\paragraph{Corrected cube and fifth roots.} By contrast, the unique normalized $g_3$ with
$q^{2}g_3^{3} = J$ has coefficient denominators
$1,1,3,1,1,9,1,1,81,1,9$ at $q^{0},\dots,q^{10}$, and the fifth root has denominators
$1,5,1,25,5,125,5,625,125,5^{6},625$. Integrality of the corrected square root is thus a
prime-specific phenomenon.

\begin{conjecture}[Dyadic integrality]\label{conj:dyadic}
The unique normalized $g$ with $qg^{2} = J$ has integer Laurent coefficients, i.e.\
$qg \in \ZZ[[q]]$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Algorithms}

All algorithms operate on truncated $1$-units over $\QQ$ to precision $P$, with exact
coefficient arithmetic.

\begin{description}
  \item[A. Corrected product.] Given $f,g$ by their $1$-units $u,v$, return $uv$: a Cauchy
  product, $O(P^{2})$ operations.
  \item[B. Corrected inverse.] Solve $uv = 1$ by $v_0 = 1$,
  $v_k = -\sum_{j=1}^{k}u_jv_{k-j}$: $O(P^{2})$, division-free, hence integrality-preserving.
  \item[C. Corrected $n$-th root by binomial substitution.] Compute $h := u-1$ and evaluate
  $B_{1/n}(h)$ by Horner's rule: $O(P^{3})$ naively, $O(P^{2}\log P)$ with repeated squaring.
  Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:divisible}.
  \item[D. Corrected $n$-th root by Newton recursion.] Solve $v^{n} = u$ coefficientwise,
  \[
    v_k = \frac{1}{n}\Big(u_k - \sum_{\substack{k_1+\cdots+k_n = k\\ k_i < k}}
      v_{k_1}\cdots v_{k_n}\Big),
  \]
  maintained efficiently via $v^{n-1}$. The explicit division by $n$ at every step is what
  makes Conjecture~\ref{conj:dyadic} surprising.
  \item[E. Rational corrected power.] For $r = p/d$, either substitute into $B_r$ or take
  the $d$-th root followed by the $p$-th power; the two agree by
  Theorem~\ref{thm:ratpow}, which doubles as a correctness check.
  \item[F. Pole-order bookkeeping.] For $m$ normalized factors the correcting exponent is
  $m-1$; computing the corrected product through $q^{N}$ requires each factor through
  $q^{N+m-1}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:determinacy}).
\end{description}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What was actually obstructed}
The phrase ``normalization obstruction'' suggests something is prevented. Nothing is: the
obstruction is a valuation, the valuation splits (Theorem~\ref{thm:structure}), and the
splitting supplies the canonical correction. The failure of closure encodes that $\Norm$ is
a \emph{torsor} rather than a subgroup --- the difference between an affine space and a
vector space. Objects normalized by a leading coefficient frequently form torsors rather
than subgroups, and the correcting twist is unique whenever the normalization condition is
cut out by a homomorphism to $\ZZ$.

\subsection{What could not be weakened}
Two reasonable-looking strengthenings are false. ``Normalized series are closed under
multiplication'' cannot be repaired by weakening the conclusion: the pole order of a product
of $m$ factors is exactly $m$, so no inequality-type statement survives; the operation must
change. And torsion freeness genuinely uses characteristic $0$: in characteristic $p$ the
$1$-units have $p$-torsion, roots exist but are not unique, and the group is not a
$\QQ$-vector space.

\subsection{Limits}
We work with formal series only; convergence, modularity and the Hauptmodul property play no
role, and correspondingly no result asserts that a corrected root of a modular function is
modular. The interest of Conjecture~\ref{conj:dyadic} is precisely that arithmetic survives
where modularity does not obviously do so.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{description}
  \item[Dyadic integrality.] Prove Conjecture~\ref{conj:dyadic}. The corrected square root
  is $B_{1/2}(qJ-1)$, so the question is whether $B_{1/2}$ maps $qJ - 1$ into
  $\ZZ_2[[q]]$. Since $B_{1/2} \notin \ZZ_2[[X]]$, integrality must come from $2$-adic
  congruences satisfied by $c(n)$ --- replication formulas in disguise.
  \item[Valuation-split rigidity.] Conjecture that the splitting
  $\CC((q))^{\times}\cong\ZZ\times\CC[[q]]^{\times}$ is rigid on the moonshine locus: any
  multiplicative map from the $194$ McKay--Thompson series commuting with Hecke-type
  operators factors as an integer valuation coordinate together with an integral $1$-unit.
  \item[The geometric mean $G$.] Determine the arithmetic of the unique $G$ with
  $q^{193}G^{194} = q^{193}\prod_g T_g$: rationality, integrality, and Galois stability
  under the action permuting conjugate McKay--Thompson series.
  \item[Weighted means.] Use the $\QQ$-vector space structure directly: for a rational
  character $\chi$ the corrected weighted product $\stbig_g T_g^{\star\chi(g)}$ is well
  defined, and its coefficients are candidates for new moonshine numerology.
  \item[Other base rings.] Over characteristic $p$ the corrected group is divisible but not
  torsion free; over $\ZZ$ it is neither. Classifying it over $\ZZ$ and $\ZZ_p$ would place
  Conjecture~\ref{conj:dyadic} inside a structural statement.
\end{description}

\section{Conclusion}

The normalization $f = q^{-1} + O(1)$ underlying Monstrous Moonshine obstructs
multiplication in a completely explicit way: pole orders add, so a product of $m$ normalized
series has a pole of order exactly $m$, and the unique repair is the factor $q^{m-1}$.
Elevating that repair to a definition --- the corrected product $f\star g = qfg$ --- turns
the normalized series into a commutative group, canonically the group of $1$-units of
$\CC[[q]]$. That group is torsion free and divisible, hence uniquely divisible, hence a
$\QQ$-vector space: roots of every order exist and are unique, rational corrected powers
obey the usual exponent laws, and the $194$ McKay--Thompson series acquire a canonical
geometric mean. The obstruction was a coordinate all along --- and it leaves behind one
sharp, numerically supported question: why the corrected square root of the modular
invariant should have integer coefficients.

